{
  "_about": "The far chart's town guard, land by land: the record's figures in place of the old chart's 1, 2 and 3 (the design director's rulings of 03:44Z to 03:50Z, 04:51Z and 05:54Z of 21 November 2026 on the historian's far-chart-town-guards.md, and of 06:03Z on its globe table, far-chart-town-guards-globe.md). A town's own guard is what stood in it with no field army and no named garrison row: police, gendarmes, home guards, volunteer town guards. Laid over siege.json only with the far side's switch on, and read by guardOf (siege.ts) alone.",
  "_form": "A land is an owner's ground on the far side of the world: all of it where the row names no places, or the provinces of the first chart it names, with the parts the chart cuts them into. 'town', 'city' and 'capital' are the guard at full for a place of that class as the game reads it (towns.ts; a village has none), in divisions' worth at the owner's own scale. The game's capital is a country's seat, so a colony's seat at the capital figure is named under 'named', as is any place the historian sets off its class; a named place has its figure whatever its class. A range is laid at its middle. Where the record gives a land no figure for a class that no place of it has, the class below's stands in the row, and its seat's for 'capital'. A figure that changed by month is a list of month and figure, as the airfield table's rows are (game.ts estAt): the figure of the last month reached stands. The post rule stays first: a post, whose record garrison is its whole defence, has no guard (economy.ts farPost). No cap by the army's size is laid on a row's figure, which is the record's size already. A guard of 0.02 or under is none, as everywhere, so the small towns of China and of the colonies have none and their cities and seats do. Far ground under no row keeps the old reckoning until the historian's tables reach it (tests/far-guards.test.ts names every such town).",
  "_scale": "Japan's lands, Britain's, the Dutch, the American, the French, Australia's and the Soviet Far East at 15,000 men to 1.0 (paper 17, and the Far East one to one). Thailand and Mongolia about the same. China at about 40,000 at a place: 0.02, 0.055, 0.1 (the director's 04:51Z, from the historian's 0.05, 0.15, 0.3). Manchukuo at its own thinner scale: 0.01, 0.03, 0.05 (from 0.03, 0.1, 0.2); Mukden, set at 0.15 beside a city's 0.1, is 0.045 by the same measure. The globe table's lands at 15,000 to 1.0, but the Soviet Union west of Chita at 0.6 of the record's figures, the scale the old chart's Soviet rows are written at (the historian's note of scale).",
  "_left": "The Dutch Landstorm is left out: the KNIL rows hold it (the director's 03:50Z). Japan's prefectures' seats stand at their class as the game reads it. Okinawa, a prefecture, and the Kurils, Hokkaido's, are laid under the home islands; Karafuto and Dairen are under no row. From the globe table: the Soviet camps' guards are left out, as the historian has it; no garrison of its section 3 is made up as guard (the director's 06:03Z); Colombo's figure is laid and the post rule, which stays first, gives it none. Basra stands at the police's 0.02 and not at 0.05 with the Iraq Levies: they are no row, but they were Britain's men on Iraq's ground, so the figure with them waits on the director. Aden stands at 0.1 with the Aden Protectorate Levies, who are no row. Pearl Harbor, a city as the game reads it, has no figure in Hawaii's row (the historian gives Honolulu and the other islands) and is under no row. The record's end dates that the table does not give (Hawaii's martial law to month 62, the Volga's destroyer battalions) are not laid: the figure stands from its month on. Under no row still: New Zealand and Samoa, Tibet, the Pacific's islands (Britain's, America's, France's, Australia's Nauru, Japan's mandate), Portuguese Timor, Karafuto, Dairen.",
